        "Peter W. Shor",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Alexei Kitaev"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how fragile quantum information can be protected in principle and helped make quantum computation a concrete physical program.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane developed landmark codes, while Kitaev's topological approach supplied a distinct and influential route to fault tolerance.",
        "maturity": "The ideas are mature and experimental implementations have progressed substantially, although large-scale fault-tolerant machines remain unrealized.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The broad development involved many contributors, and the relationship between foundational theory and eventual practical impact remains in progress."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controlled quantum many-body systems experimentally accessible, enabling direct study of models that are difficult to solve or realize in materials.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner led defining experimental demonstrations, while Zoller helped establish the theoretical framework and vision for quantum simulation.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been productive for decades and remains an active, increasingly capable experimental tool.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The work is a broad program with important contributions from Cirac and many other experimental groups."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "John D. Joannopoulos",
        "Sajeev John"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals established how periodic structures can control light through photonic band gaps, creating a major field in optics and photonics.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently introduced key ideas, and Joannopoulos developed the theory and helped make the subject coherent and usable.",
        "maturity": "The concept has been extensively confirmed and has influenced optical devices and nanophotonics over several decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The path from the original proposals to practical devices involved a wide community, and the relative credit for the field's later impact is diffuse."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michel Della Negra",
        "Peter Jenni",
        "Tejinder S. Virdee"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The ATLAS and CMS discovery of the Higgs boson was a landmark experimental result, completing a central test of the Standard Model.",
        "attribution": "These leaders were instrumental in building the experiments and directing the discovery effort; the result depended on large international collaborations.",
        "maturity": "The discovery is robustly confirmed by multiple production and decay channels and subsequent precision measurements.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The individual credit problem is unusually difficult for a collaboration-based discovery, and the theoretical prediction and experimental observation are separate contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Bennett",
        "Lyman A. Page Jr.",
        "David N. Spergel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "WMAP's precise mapping of the cosmic microwave background turned cosmology into a precision science and tightly constrained the universe's age, composition, and history.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Page led the instrument and mission science, while Spergel played a central role in interpreting the results and extracting cosmological parameters.",
        "maturity": "The measurements have been repeatedly tested and remain foundational, with later observations refining rather than overturning the picture.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The result rests on a large mission team and on a broader sequence of CMB measurements, making individual attribution and scope consequential."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Artur Ekert",
        "Charles H. Bennett",
        "Gilles Brassard"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum key distribution connected foundational quantum principles to secure communication and helped establish quantum information as a field with both physical depth and practical reach.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Brassard's protocol and Ekert's entanglement-based approach were distinct foundational contributions.",
        "maturity": "The principles are experimentally well established and systems have been deployed, though distance, rate, and implementation security remain active engineering challenges.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The achievement spans protocols, security proofs, and technology, with substantial later contributions beyond these originators."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B023",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped-ion quantum computation established a remarkably precise route to coherent control of individual quantum systems and enabled important demonstrations of quantum logic.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller proposed the influential gate scheme, and Blatt's experiments demonstrated and extended trapped-ion quantum logic.",
        "maturity": "The platform has achieved high-fidelity control and sophisticated small-scale operations, while scaling to useful fault-tolerant computation remains unfinished.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The foundational proposals and experimental realization involved many contributors, and the ultimate impact of this particular architecture is still developing."
        ]
      }
    }
